body:
An oversized queued acquire can block a lower-priority unlock waiter. Cancelling that acquire removes the blocking queue entry, but the unlock waiter is not reconsidered.

The semaphore has available capacity and is not locked, yet the existing `waitForUnlock` promise stays pending.

**Reproduction**

```js
const { Semaphore } = require('async-mutex');

(async () => {
  const semaphore = new Semaphore(1);
  const blockedAcquire = semaphore.acquire(2).catch(() => {});

  let resolved = false;
  const waiter = semaphore.waitForUnlock(1).then(() => {
    resolved = true;
  });

  semaphore.cancel();
  await blockedAcquire;
  await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 20));

  console.log(semaphore.getValue()); // 1
  console.log(semaphore.isLocked()); // false
  console.log(resolved);             // false

  semaphore.setValue(1);
  await waiter;
  console.log(resolved); // true, after explicitly resetting the same value
})().catch(console.error);
```

**Expected:** Once the blocking queue is cancelled, a waiter whose requested weight and priority can now be satisfied should resolve. No capacity change should be necessary.

**Suggested fix:** Re-evaluate eligible unlock waiters after cancelling queued acquisitions, while respecting any capacity still held by active acquisitions.

**Version:** 0.5.0. **Environment:** Node.js v24.19.0.
